# Line endings are LF everywhere — in the repo AND in the working copy.
#
# Without this file Git falls back to the machine's core.autocrlf, which on
# Git for Windows defaults to true: check out CRLF, commit LF. Editors and
# tooling then rewrite files as LF, so the working copy drifts into a mix
# (this repo had 9 CRLF files against 391 LF) and Git reports dozens of files
# as modified when nothing changed — `git diff --ignore-all-space` on them is
# empty. It also prints "LF will be replaced by CRLF" on every commit.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working copy too, so the setting no longer depends on who
# cloned it or how their global config is set. CI, the deploy and every script
# run on Linux, so LF is what everything downstream expects anyway.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.zip binary
*.mdb binary
